  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "The extended sine rule establishes a connection between the sides, angles and circumradius $R$ of a triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p28-q18-diagram",
        "role": "question",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 28, p28-q18-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.8pt,every node/.style={font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,text=black}]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (O) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (A) at (192:2);\n\\coordinate (B) at (348:2);\n\\coordinate (C) at (62:2);\n\\draw[black] (O) circle (2);\n\\draw[black] (A)--node[midway,above left] {$b$} (C)--node[midway,right] {$a$} (B)--node[midway,below] {$c$} (A);\n\\draw[black] (O)--node[midway,right] {$R$} (C);\n\\fill[black] (O) circle (1.2pt);\n\\node[left] at (O) {$O$};\n\\node[left] at (A) {$A$};\n\\node[right] at (B) {$B$};\n\\node[above] at (C) {$C$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 47.5,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 28,
          "sourceRegion": "Upper right column, question 18",
          "description": "Unshaded circle with inscribed triangle ABC, horizontal base AB, marked centre O and radius OC.",
          "mathematics": "AB=c, AC=b, BC=a; OC=R. All three vertices lie on the circle. Coordinates express the supplied topology without adding numerical givens.",
          "answerVisibility": "All labels and the radius are permanent student-visible givens."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p28-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Assuming the sine rule, prove that\n$$\\frac{a}{\\sin A}=\\frac{b}{\\sin B}=\\frac{c}{\\sin C}=2R.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{a}{\\sin A}=\\dfrac{b}{\\sin B}=\\dfrac{c}{\\sin C}=2R$.\n\nBisect the central angle subtended by $BC$, then apply the sine rule.",
          "worked": "Join $O$ to $B$ and let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$. Since $OB=OC=R$, $OM$ bisects $\\angle BOC$ and is perpendicular to $BC$. The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference, so $\\angle BOC=2A$ and $\\angle BOM=A$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin A&=\\frac{BM}{OB}\\\\\\sin A&=\\frac{a}{2R}\\\\\\frac{a}{\\sin A}&=2R.\\end{align*}$$\nThe sine rule now gives\n$$\\frac{a}{\\sin A}=\\frac{b}{\\sin B}=\\frac{c}{\\sin C}=2R.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that\n$$\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\frac{abc}{4R}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\dfrac{abc}{4R}$.\n\nSubstitute $\\sin C=\\dfrac{c}{2R}$ into the triangle area rule.",
          "worked": "From part (a), $\\sin C=\\dfrac{c}{2R}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)&=\\frac12 ab\\sin C\\\\\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)&=\\frac12 ab\\left(\\frac{c}{2R}\\right)\\\\\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)&=\\frac{abc}{4R}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If $r$ is the inradius of $\\triangle ABC$, then show that\n$$\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\frac{(a+b+c)r}{2}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\dfrac{(a+b+c)r}{2}$.\n\nJoin the incentre to the vertices and add the three triangle areas.",
          "worked": "Join the incentre to $A$, $B$ and $C$. The three resulting triangles have bases $a$, $b$ and $c$, each with perpendicular height $r$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)&=\\frac12 ar+\\frac12 br+\\frac12 cr\\\\\\operatorname{area}(\\triangle ABC)&=\\frac{(a+b+c)r}{2}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 37
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that\n$$rR=\\frac{abc}{2(a+b+c)}.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$rR=\\dfrac{abc}{2(a+b+c)}$.\n\nEquate the area formulas from parts (b) and (c).",
          "worked": "Parts (b) and (c) give\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{(a+b+c)r}{2}&=\\frac{abc}{4R}\\\\(a+b+c)rR&=\\frac{abc}{2}\\\\rR&=\\frac{abc}{2(a+b+c)}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      }
    ]
  },
